#note: The third verb in this line is problematic. It is here taken to be a form of @i{kalû} N “to be held back”, for lack of a better option. The Old Babylonian parallel (OB I b+10) offers @akk{a-ka-AD} of unclear etymology (see the note to that line). For the first-millennium version, @bib{RN262@318 ad 88} suggests to read @akk{a-ka-ad!}, i.e. @i{akkad}, derived from @i{nakādu} “to beat; to be frightened”. As she admits herself, this form should be @i{akkud}. @bib{RN482@554} proposes to understand it as a form of “@i{akālu}”, i.e. @i{ekēlu} “to be dark”, a reading with similar problems concerning the verb’s vowels.
// cf. L I.12 SB "-" 156
